{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":false,"decision":"fail","reasons":["security.status_not_clean"],"slug":"who-is-actor","displayName":"Who Is Actor","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wscats/skills/who-is-actor","publisherHandle":"wscats","publisherDisplayName":"enoyao","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wscats","version":"1.0.15","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1780389533323,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/who-is-actor/card?ownerHandle=wscats&version=1.0.15","sha256":"e2605641a013c3697d165a107021c34a9943dc9c142a6cc7618125c8a0745b10","size":2347,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"8a3c5b9fdd6e78a280a6460201b757d1020e19d740583f6e07b311eee12a2ed1","bundleFingerprints":["5b79315a63815667e19dab84dc3e993a7838edb16bbf38fbad7eda0db8e002d5","9d6b29fbc9509758ea9dd8fcd0158e7176c00acccd941861279a90de77dbc6b0"],"files":[{"path":"INTRO.md","size":15784,"sha256":"5db1478b6bc199419659bb76cf1ecb9951fe90a240c35342cec0ed8c12bf2f14","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"README.md","size":15694,"sha256":"a12672a03236b2aa7434051656626eb9bb33063fe045378567559e2b7c6c433e","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":51727,"sha256":"c4d9493760650b366f61a6fcb9dad7ccdac4d0341a18473b9ea19a9ecf74b149","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"SKILL_CN.md","size":45792,"sha256":"be3b0334c8cd2f5c567120a18c581ac4e51fe00e601e4dd10e3d01aa9b1c69d8","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"publish.sh","size":1745,"sha256":"81674b50ac571ce3b12a0c7e8be94539bfb8b6a72a62227ce80a7edd7bbeae4f","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill.yaml","size":5759,"sha256":"eb8bf00eef5775f6edca403f2768f2dcf668c78ffbb2d7671f7cefe49cfb3b04","contentType":"text/yaml"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"suspicious","passed":false,"rawStatus":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This repository-analysis skill is mostly purpose-aligned, but its documented workflows can expose raw repository paths and have command-scoping gaps despite stronger privacy claims.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789174244798,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Raw repository paths can cross the declared local-only privacy boundary"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":449,"endLine":490}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"f3d6a1e1f9a5033f"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `SKILL.md:449-490`; duplicated in `SKILL_CN.md:425-461`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Sensitive information exposure through incomplete local aggregation  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\nThe rework command returns dates and up to 500 raw repository paths without implementing the required reduction or anonymization:\n\n```bash\n# Per-day, per-file modification trace across the WHOLE repository.\n# IMPORTANT: the raw output below contains full file paths and is LOCAL-ONLY.\n# The agent MUST reduce it locally to a single repository-wide rework ratio\n# (count of (file, day) pairs where the same file is touched again within a\n# 7-day window divided by the total touch count) and a file-extension\n# histogram, BEFORE any value crosses into a model prompt. If per-file\n# grouping must be retained, replace each path with an opaque ID such as\n# `file_<sha1[:8]>`.\ngit -C <repo_path> log --pretty=format:\"%ad\" --date=short --name-only | head -500\n```\n\nThe bus-factor pipeline removes author names but still emits full paths before the subsequently described sanitization step:\n\n```bash\ngit -C <repo_path> log --pretty=format:'__AUTHOR__%an' --name-only \\\n  | awk '\n      /^__AUTHOR__/ { author = substr($0, 11); next }\n      NF > 0        { key = $0 SUBSEP author;\n                      if (!(key in seen)) { seen[key] = 1; cnt[$0]++ } }\n      END           { for (f in cnt) if (cnt[f] == 1) print f \"\\t1\" }\n    ' \\\n  | head -30\n```\n\nThe instructions then state:\n\n```text\nThe output of this pipeline is <file_path>\\t1 — file path plus the integer\n1 (distinct historical author count). The path is then sanitised per the\nfile-path filtering rules ... before any value crosses into a report or\nAI prompt.\n```\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe Skill defines full file paths as local-only sensitive data and claims that only numeric aggregates, extension histograms, and opaque identifiers may enter model context. The documented commands do not enforce that boundary.\n\nIn an instruction-driven Skill, commands are normally executed through an agent tool. Tool output may be returned directly to the agent and become part of model context. The rework command's final output is raw path data because `head` only limits the number of lines. The bus-factor pipeline similarly emits full paths and relies on an unspecified later sanitization stage. Once a tool has returned those paths, later filtering cannot undo their prior disclosure to the model provider.\n\nRepository paths can reveal:\n\n- Customer or tenant names\n- Internal ticket or incident identifiers\n- Employee or account names\n- Proprietary architecture and component names\n- Security-sensitive filenames\n- Credentials embedded in filenames\n- Confidential product and project information\n\nThis conflicts with the stronger privacy claims in `README.md`, `INTRO.md`, and `skill.yaml` that full paths are never forwarded to the AI model.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. An authorized user asks the Skill to analyze a repository containing confidential filenames.\n2. The agent follows the documented rework or bus-factor workflow.\n3. `git log --name-only` produces full paths from repository history.\n4. The final `head` or `awk | head` stage leaves those paths in command output.\n5. The execution tool returns that output to the agent.\n6. Raw repository paths enter model context before the described subsequent sanitization can occur.\n\nNo malicious repository content is required; ordinary confidential filenames are sufficient to trigger the disclosure.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe vulnerability can disclose repository structure and filename-derived sensitive information to the AI service processing tool output. It does not grant write access, operating-system privileges, or access outside the selected repository. However, it exceeds the minimum disclosure necessary to calculate aggregate rework statistics.\n\nThe affected scope includes path data from the selected Git history, including paths that have since been deleted from the working tree. The `head -500` and `head -30` limits reduce volume but do not provide confidentiality.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Replace the rework example with a complete local aggregation pipeline whose final output contains only numeric totals and extension-level statistics.\n2. Hash or tokenize each path inside the same trusted local process before any output is returned to the agent.\n3. Do not document raw-path output followed by a later filtering step. Filtering must occur before the execution boundary returns stdout.\n4. Default bus-factor reporting to opaque file identifiers. Require explicit, informed user opt-in before displaying sanitized paths.\n5. Apply sensitive-path exclusion before output, including case-insensitive matching for `.env`, `.credentials`, secrets, passwords, tokens, certificates, and private-key extensions.\n6. Use a trusted local wrapper with a fixed output schema rather than relying on the model to transform raw command output.\n7. Update `SKILL_CN.md` with the equivalent corrected pipelines.\n8. Revise `README.md`, `INTRO.md`, and `skill.yaml` so their privacy claims precisely reflect the enforceable implementation.\n9. Add a test repository containing sensitive filenames and verify that no tool response or model-bound trace contains the original paths.\n]]>"}}]},{"ruleId":"T09","level":"warning","message":{"text":"Branch values can be interpreted as Git options and repository paths are not safely quoted"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":208,"endLine":229}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"d1a7d954bcba99a8"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n## Vulnerability Details\n**File Location**: `SKILL.md:208-229, 374-488`; duplicated in `SKILL_CN.md:191-212, 356-459`  \n**Vulnerability Type**: Git option injection and unsafe shell argument construction  \n**Risk Level**: Medium\n\n### Vulnerable Code\n\nBranch validation permits a leading hyphen:\n\n```text\n4. **`branch` (Branch Name) Validation:**\n   - Only allowed characters: letters, digits, `/`, `-`, `_`, `.`\n   - Regex whitelist: `^[a-zA-Z0-9/_.-]+$`\n   - Must NOT contain the `..` substring\n   - If validation fails, use the default branch and warn the user\n```\n\nThe workflow instructs the agent to append the branch to Git commands:\n\n```text\nAll commands below are repository-scoped and aggregate (no `--author=`\nfilter). Append `--since`, `--until`, and `<branch>` if the user specified\na date range or branch.\n```\n\nCommand examples also use the repository path as an unquoted shell placeholder:\n\n```bash\ngit -C <repo_path> log --pretty=format:\"%aI\" | cut -c12-13 | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn\n\ngit -C <repo_path> log --pretty=tformat: --numstat \\\n  | awk '{ add += $1; subs += $2 } END { printf \"added: %s, deleted: %s\\n\", add, subs }'\n\ngit -C <repo_path> log --pretty=format:\"%ad\" --date=short --name-only | head -500\n```\n\nThe path validation allows spaces, while the command examples do not quote `<repo_path>`.\n\nDate validation also checks only lexical shape:\n\n```text\n3. **`since` / `until` (Date Parameters) Validation:**\n   - Must match ISO date format: `^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$`\n   - If validation fails, ignore the parameter and warn the user\n```\n\nThis accepts semantically invalid dates such as `2024-99-99`.\n\n### Technical Analysis\n\nThe branch allowlist is insufficient because it validates characters but not argument semantics. Values such as the following satisfy the regex and do not contain `..`:\n\n```text\n--all\n--patch\n-p\n--stat\n```\n\nIf one of these values is appended to `git log`, Git may interpret it as an option rather than a revision. For example:\n\n- `--all` broadens analysis to all refs instead of the requested branch.\n- `--patch` or `-p` can cause patch content to be emitted.\n- Other allowed `git log` options can alter output volume and expose additional repository information.\n\nThis is option injection rather than arbitrary shell command injection: dangerous shell metacharacters are blocked, but attacker-controlled input can still change Git's behavior.\n\nSeparately, `git -C <repo_path>` is unsafe for valid paths containing whitespace. Shell tokenization can split the path into multiple arguments, causing incorrect targeting, unexpected option interpretation, or command failure. This contradicts the verification requirement that all user-supplied values be quoted.\n\nThe date issue is a validation correctness defect. It can produce errors or unexpected Git date interpretation, although it is less directly exploitable than branch option injection.\n\n### Attack Path\n\n1. A user supplies a branch value such as `--all` or `--patch`.\n2. The value passes `^[a-zA-Z0-9/_.-]+$` and the `..` check.\n3. The agent appends the value to a documented `git log` command.\n4. Git parses it as an option rather than as a branch revision.\n5. The command analyzes additional refs or emits more sensitive output than intended.\n6. The expanded output can enter the aggregation pipeline or tool response.\n\nFor the path-handling defect:\n\n1. A legitimate repository path contains spaces.\n2. The agent substitutes it into the unquoted `<repo_path>` placeholder.\n3. The shell splits the path into multiple arguments.\n4. Git either analyzes an unintended argument, interprets a component as an option, or fails unpredictably.\n\n### Impact Assessment\n\nThe branch issue can broaden access from the intended branch to additional local refs and can cause patch or metadata output beyond the declared minimum scope. It does not independently provide operating-system command execution because shell metacharacters are rejected, and it does not grant repository write access.\n\nThe maximum affected scope is Git data already locally readable by the invoking agent in the selected repository, including other branches and refs. Combined with the raw-output privacy issue, option injection can increase the quantity and sensitivity of information returned to model context.\n\nThe unquoted-path defect primarily affects command integrity and repository targeting. It may cause denial of analysis or accidental processing of unintended arguments but does not, under the documented character restrictions alone, establish arbitrary shell execution.\n]]>","severity":"Medium"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n## Remediation Suggestions\n\n1. Reject every branch value beginning with `-`.\n2. Treat user input as structured command arguments rather than interpolated shell text.\n3. Verify revisions explicitly using a narrowly approved command such as:\n\n   ```bash\n   git -C \"$repo_path\" rev-parse --verify --end-of-options \"$branch^{commit}\"\n   ```\n\n   The command whitelist must be deliberately updated if this form is adopted.\n\n4. Use `--end-of-options` where supported and ensure revisions are inserted only in revision positions.\n5. Quote repository paths in every example:\n\n   ```bash\n   git -C \"$repo_path\" log ...\n   ```\n\n6. Prefer argument arrays in the host implementation so values never undergo shell word splitting.\n7. Keep branch, date, and path values separate from pipeline source text; do not construct commands through string concatenation.\n8. Validate dates semantically with a trusted date parser, including month lengths and leap years, rather than relying only on a regular expression.\n9. Add negative tests for `--all`, `--patch`, `-p`, paths containing spaces, and impossible dates.\n10. Apply the same changes to `SKILL_CN.md` and ensure dry-run examples display exact quoting and option-boundary handling.\n]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":50,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789174064.84879,"endTime":1789174201.6101186}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"who-is-actor","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v57fep4hxaza84g9z8b8g72k5h8e9nab-PspyCp/clawscan-skillspector-3217597345/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-12T00:47:43.899360+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","max_issue_severity":"HIGH"},"components":[{"path":"INTRO.md","type":"markdown","lines":238,"executable":false,"size_bytes":15784,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"README.md","type":"markdown","lines":272,"executable":false,"size_bytes":15694,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":647,"executable":false,"size_bytes":51727,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"SKILL_CN.md","type":"markdown","lines":617,"executable":false,"size_bytes":45792,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"publish.sh","type":"shell","lines":52,"executable":true,"size_bytes":1745,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"skill.yaml","type":"yaml","lines":142,"executable":false,"size_bytes":5759,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-cc034b1a97ce4cf385b0feaacf3c4180","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":19,"end_line":19},"finding":null,"explanation":"The README asserts that no contributor display names appear, but elsewhere it discusses contributor-specific outputs and examples, creating a material mismatch in privacy and scope guarantees. This can mislead users into authorizing analysis under false assumptions and enable downstream use of contributor-identifiable data despite the stated aggregate-only design.","remediation":"Make the documentation internally consistent: either remove all contributor-level language/examples and guarantee aggregate-only outputs, or explicitly disclose any contributor-level processing/display that occurs. Add a single authoritative privacy/scope section stating exactly what identifiers may be shown, and align README, manifest, and skill behavior to that contract.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":19,"end_line":19}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-e2cba2967dd3470ca9bfa815ce72f40e","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":40,"end_line":40},"finding":null,"explanation":"Describing contributor-level analysis conflicts with the declared repository-level-only purpose and increases the chance that the skill will be used for profiling, ranking, or personnel inference. In a commit-history analysis skill, that mismatch is especially sensitive because Git metadata directly maps activity patterns to individuals.","remediation":"Remove contributor-level analysis descriptions and examples, and restrict outputs to repository-level aggregates only. Add explicit refusal logic and tests to block requests for individual comparison, ranking, or per-person reporting, and update the README to reflect those enforced limitations.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":40,"end_line":40}]},{"id":"SDI-1","finding_id":"finding-e92c21b14273469284091e327e423e46","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.96,"location":{"file":"README.md","start_line":173,"end_line":203},"finding":null,"explanation":"Contributor-specific examples and narratives normalize per-person interpretation and undermine the manifest's aggregate-only, no-display-name scope. Even as examples, they teach operators to read the tool as a personnel-analysis aid, increasing privacy risk and the likelihood of misuse against individual contributors.","remediation":"Delete or rewrite all contributor-specific examples into repository-level or fully anonymized aggregate examples. Ensure examples never mention single contributors, ownership by one person, or individual activity narratives, and add review checks so future documentation cannot drift from the aggregate-only policy.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":[],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"README.md","start_line":173,"end_line":203}]},{"id":"TP4","finding_id":"finding-cbafd41d63a1424894f166b92989d9c0","category":"MCP Tool Poisoning","pattern":null,"severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"There is a clear description-behavior mismatch. The declared purpose describes a repository-analysis skill using local git and Unix tools to compute aggregate collaboration metrics under strict privacy constraints. The actual code chunk does none of that: it is an operational publish.sh script for packaging/deploying the skill to ClawHub. It checks for the clawhub CLI, verifies authentication with clawhub whoami/login, and runs clawhub publish. These are materially different capabilities and involve different resources and triggers than the declared repository-analysis function. This is not merely a supporting implementation detail of the analysis itself; it is separate deployment infrastructure.","remediation":"Update the skill description to accurately reflect all capabilities, or remove undeclared functionality from the implementation.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI02","AML.T0080","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{"code_path":"publish.sh","code_start_line":1,"code_end_line":52,"actual_behavior_summary":"The supplied code is a Bash publishing script that checks for the ClawHub CLI, verifies login status, and publishes the skill package to ClawHub with a version/tag. It performs deployment/authentication actions, not repository analysis."},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":1,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-81c55e1177104c75a800b837e1e220d9","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":303,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -rf /\"          -> agent MUST reject (dangerous characters)\n   \"Analyze just author Alice\"            -> agent MUST decline per-author scoping and offer repository-level analysis instead\n   \"Analyz","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"\n2. **Input validation test:** Deliberately provide invalid inputs and verify rejection:\n   ```\n   \"Analyze /tmp/test; rm -rf /\"          -> agent MUST reject (dangerous characters)\n   \"Analyze just author Alice\"            -> agent MUST decline per-author scoping and offer repository-level analysis instead\n   \"Analyze since 2024-13-99\"             -> agent MUST reject or warn (invalid date)\n   \"Analyze branch ../../etc/passwd\"      -> agent MUST reject (.. not allowed)","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"55602dd1c0995720a08d70c379fae884e20d99729b7a195fc965bfafd6c963a8","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":303,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-63d07d39edbf479e9b72930fdada7d24","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":303,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -rf /","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"\n2. **输入校验测试：** 故意提供无效输入并验证拒绝：\n   ```\n   \"分析 /tmp/test; rm -rf /\"          → 代理必须拒绝（危险字符）\n   \"只分析作者 Alice\"                  → 代理必须拒绝按作者缩窄范围，改为提供仓库级分析\n   \"分析 since 2024-13-99\"             → 代理必须拒绝或警告（无效日期）\n   \"分析分支 ../../etc/passwd\"          → 代理必须拒绝（不允许 ..）","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"f3ff4d6cfbe1b7f6b23dd4d1379edae984c14738c5f5b14db40e215beb673f08","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":303,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-63d07d39edbf479e9b72930fdada7d24","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"SKILL_CN.md","start_line":285,"end_line":null},"finding":"rm -rf /","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"\n2. **输入校验测试：** 故意提供